{
    "title": "Designating Fentanyl as a Weapon of Mass Destruction",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 18, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-18/pdf/2025-23417.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has issued an order that labels illicit fentanyl and its key ingredients as weapons of mass destruction, signaling a shift from treating fentanyl mainly as a drug problem to treating it as a national security and public safety threat.\n\nThe order directs a wide federal response. The Justice Department is told to step up investigations and prosecutions, including seeking tougher penalties where appropriate. The State and Treasury Departments are instructed to go after money and assets tied to the fentanyl trade, including actions involving banks. The department responsible for the armed forces is asked, alongside the Attorney General, to consider providing resources to support law enforcement, and to update military guidance on responding to chemical incidents at home to include fentanyl. Homeland Security is directed to map fentanyl smuggling networks using intelligence tools typically used for monitoring weapons and proliferation. The order defines \u201cillicit fentanyl\u201d as fentanyl made or moved in violation of federal drug laws and names core precursor chemicals, such as piperidone. It also notes that all actions must follow existing laws and budgets and that the order does not create new legal rights for private parties.\n\nWho stands to benefit? Communities harmed by overdoses may see a more forceful effort to disrupt supply chains. Law enforcement, border personnel, and first responders could gain clearer authority and more tools. Families and public health advocates focused on preventing deaths may welcome stronger action against organized groups that traffic fentanyl and its ingredients.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Civil liberties and criminal justice advocates may worry about expanded penalties, use of military-linked resources in domestic enforcement, and the potential for overreach. Hospitals, researchers, and chemical manufacturers using legitimate fentanyl or related chemicals could face added scrutiny or disruptions. Financial institutions and cross-border businesses may encounter new compliance burdens. Some may also question whether a security-first approach could shift attention or funding away from treatment and prevention, or complicate international cooperation.",
